# Cursor instruction (consult before making changes)

Cursor must consult both this file (.cursorrules) and .cursor/rules/*.mdc before making changes. If conflict exists, prefer the stricter rule.

## HARD RULES (apply to all future Cursor work)
- Do NOT delete, rename, or "clean up" any files unless the user explicitly requests it.
- Do NOT refactor unrelated code "for quality" or "style".
- Make the smallest possible diff to achieve the requested outcome.
- If you need to change multiple independent things, split into separate commits.
- Never modify already-applied Supabase migrations. If a migration timestamp exists in `supabase_migrations.schema_migrations`, treat that file as immutable and create a new timestamped migration instead.
- Check if a migration timestamp is applied: `SELECT 1 FROM supabase_migrations.schema_migrations WHERE version = '<timestamp>';` (run in Dashboard SQL Editor). If it returns a row, do not edit that migration file; create a new timestamped migration instead.
- If a change would touch >5 files OR delete/rename any file, STOP and ask before proceeding.
- Before creating a branch, run `git status --porcelain`. If any unrelated paths are dirty, STOP and ask whether to `git restore` or `git stash`. Do not proceed until clean.

## Safer default Git workflow
- Never work directly on main. main is for integration only.
- Before starting any task:
  - `git fetch origin --prune`
  - `git checkout main`
  - `git pull --ff-only origin main` (or equivalent ff-only)
  - `git status --porcelain` must be empty; if not, STOP and ask whether to restore or stash (do not proceed).
- Create a new branch per task: `fix/<short-desc>`, `feat/<short-desc>`, `chore/<short-desc>`.
- Atomic commits:
  - `git add -p`
  - Review with `git diff --cached`
  - One logical change per commit; no mixed concerns.
  - Commit message prefixes: `fix:`, `feat:`, `chore:`, `test:`, `docs:`
  - Push after each atomic commit: `git push -u origin <branch>` on first push, then `git push`.
- Never create merge commits on main from pulling: use fast-forward only (`pull --ff-only`).
- If a non-fast-forward push happens (e.g. push rejected): STOP and ask; do not merge origin/main into local main automatically.
- Keep main stable: main should always pass `npm test` (and lint/type-check where applicable). Merge back via squash if the branch has many small commits, otherwise merge normally.

### Non-fast-forward / diverged branch playbook
- If main and origin/main have diverged, Cursor must STOP and not choose automatically.
- Run: `git fetch origin` then `git log --oneline --decorate --graph --left-right main...origin/main`.
- Then ask the user whether to:
  - (A) merge or rebase on a feature branch, or
  - (B) reset main to origin/main (e.g. with a backup branch first).
- Cursor must not choose (A) or (B) for the user.

## Pre-commit hygiene (required for atomic pushes)
- Before staging, check what changed: `git status --porcelain` and `git diff --name-only`.
- Stage only the minimum needed hunks: `git add -p`.
- Before committing, review staged diff only: `git diff --cached`.
- If unrelated files are modified, stash them (keep index): `git stash push -u --keep-index -m "WIP unrelated"`.
- Never mix concerns in one commit (e.g. DB + UI + tests) unless they are inseparable for the same fix.

## Atomic Git + Branch workflow
- Always create a new branch per task:
  - `fix/<short-desc>` for bug fixes
  - `feat/<short-desc>` for features
  - `chore/<short-desc>` for tooling/docs
- Make atomic commits:
  - One logical change per commit (no mixed concerns)
  - Prefer `git add -p` to stage only relevant hunks
  - Commit message prefixes: `fix:`, `feat:`, `chore:`, `test:`, `docs:`
- Push after each atomic commit (so we can revert easily):
  - `git push -u origin <branch>` for first push; then `git push` after each commit
- Keep main stable:
  - main should always pass `npm test` (and lint/type-check where applicable)
  - Merge back via squash if the branch has many small commits, otherwise merge normally

## Quality gates (lightweight)
- For mobile app changes, run (or keep changes compatible with):
  - `cd mobile && npm run lint -- --max-warnings 0`
  - `cd mobile && npm run type-check`
  - `cd mobile && npm test`
  - `cd mobile && npm run format:check`
- Prefer fixing root-cause rather than skipping suites.

## Supabase / DB safety
- RLS: `upsert` with `onConflict` can trigger UPDATE, so an UPDATE policy is required for that path.
- PostgREST schema cache: when adding or changing functions used as RPC, include `SELECT pg_notify('pgrst','reload schema');` in the migration.
- Function return type changes: do not replace an existing function's RETURNS TABLE shape in-place. Create a `_v2` function and switch the client to it.
- Check if a migration timestamp is applied: `SELECT 1 FROM supabase_migrations.schema_migrations WHERE version = '<timestamp>';` (run in Dashboard SQL Editor). If it returns a row, do not edit that migration file; create a new timestamped migration instead.

## Branch hygiene (keep repo manageable)
- Keep at most 3 active WIP branches at a time. Everything else must be merged or archived/deleted.
- Before starting new work:
  - `git fetch origin --prune`
  - `git branch -vv` (review active branches)
- Categorize each branch:
  - READY → open PR and squash merge; then delete local + remote branch.
  - WIP → ensure it is pushed; rename to `wip/<topic>` if not ready to merge.
  - ABANDONED → tag as `archive/<branch>` (optional), then delete local + remote.
- Never delete a branch without first confirming it's merged or tagged/archived.
- Never merge into `main` locally; use PR + squash merge by default.

